<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Thapsus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Thapsus was fought on 6 April 46 BCE near Thapsus, on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and the Pompeian forces of Metellus Scipio, supported by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>. Caesar's decisive victory destroyed the republican cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and Cato, Juba and other leaders took their own lives. Caesar then annexed much of Numidia as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa-nova">Africa Nova</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus.jpg" alt="Battle of Thapsus" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Thapsus,_46_BC_(Initial_deployment_of_troops).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his victory over the Pompeians and Juba I at the <b>Battle of Thapsus</b> in 46 BCE, Julius Caesar annexed much of Numidia as a second province, Africa Nova, so that the old province became Africa Vetus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Thapsus</b> in 46 BCE Caesar defeated them, and Juba, refused entry to his own town of Zama, died in a suicide pact with the Roman general Marcus Petreius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cato the Younger killed himself there in 46 BCE after Caesar's victory at <b>Thapsus</b>, and the city merged into Africa Proconsularis.</blockquote><a href="/state/utica">Utica</a></li></ul></section>
